- Which metrics define success for resuming traffic, and how are tenants verified?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical, beginner-friendly observability implementation: instrumenting a new service end-to-end with minimal changes and validating traces in a production UI.

## Key Concepts
- OpenTelemetry auto-instrumentation
- Trace-context propagation and log correlation
- Lightweight instrumentation for HTTP and database spans
- OTLP exporter configuration and backend dashboard validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Node example: enable auto-instrumentation
// Install: @opentelemetry/api @opentelemetry/sdk-node @opentelemetry/instrumentation-http
import { NodeSDK } from '@opentelemetry/sdk-node';
import { getNodeAutoInstrumentations } from '@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node';

const sdk = new NodeSDK({
  instrumentations: [getNodeAutoInstrumentations()],
  serviceName: 'accounts-service',
  traceExporter: new OTLPTraceExporter()
});
sdk.start();

// Trace ID in logs
import { trace } from '@opentelemetry/api';
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const traceId = trace.getActiveSpan()?.spanContext().traceId;
  console.log(`Request ${req.method} ${req.path} [trace=${traceId}]`);
  next();
});
```

## Validation Plan
- Configure OTLP exporter to your backend (Jaeger/Tempo/Grafana)
- Verify HTTP request spans appear in dashboard
- Confirm database query spans are captured
- Check trace IDs appear in structured logs
- Test end-to-end with synthetic request and validate trace flow

## Why This Is Asked

Tests translating a deployment-induced resource bottleneck into a practical incident plan with observable signals, actionable thresholds, safe mitigations, and a clear rollback path.

## Key Concepts

- DB connection pool sizing and tuning
- Observability: pool metrics, wait events, latency, CPU
- Safe backpressure and traffic shaping
- Rollback vs hotfix and feature flags

## Code Example

```javascript
// simple circuit breaker
class CB {
  constructor() { this.failures = 0; this.state = 'CLOSED'; }
  allow() { return this.state !== 'OPEN'; }
  onFailure() { this.failures++; if (this.failures > 5) this.state = 'OPEN'; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure pool utilization in a typical Postgres + Node.js app?
- What are the trade-offs of increasing max connections vs using a proxy like PgBouncer?
